It was like one of the smoothest procedures I had ever been part of. I was like, man, this is, this is amazing.

132
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Yeah, like anything you could do to like, yeah, to not have to manage all those steps just to like offload it onto someone or something else. I think that's hugely valuable for us.

133
00:27:51,000 --> 00:28:01,000
Yeah, checklists are so valuable. The important thing is making sure that you read everything on the checklist or else forget what's going on.

134
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:11,000
And I've been there, you know, with myself or my partner, like, you know, we were kind of running running through the checklist and we're not reading it out loud.

135
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:27,000
And because you didn't read it out loud, you're like, oh, I missed that. But yeah, reading that checklist out loud, maybe even handing it off to, you know, one of the EMT basics or one of the ambulance personnel just read this out loud from, from top to bottom.

136
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:35,000
Make sure we have everything in place and, and you will not forget anything. So, super, super important.

137
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:48,000
Yeah, I have a low stakes example of that is that I have a checklist of like things to pack before I go to work because I like I go to wait to work for two weeks and then I'm off so like, I gotta like take my whole life with me.

138
00:28:48,000 --> 00:29:00,000
And I have a checklist that like on my phone that I click through to make sure I have everything. And this last time I went to work, I went through the checklist and I was just like pencil, lipping it and it was like, I made the checklist and it's for myself.

139
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:09,000
And I was like, I preach about doing checklists all the time, and I still miss something so I got to work as a hand. Where is this thing?

140
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:13,000
I didn't follow my checklist.

141
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:16,000
Yeah, for sure.

142
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:32,000
Along these ideas, getting back this idea of like having a script and knowing that it like can help you work through complicated things. Do you have an example maybe of when this may have helped you in the past like before you started working on this tool?

143
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:56,000
Well, I have a very embarrassing and good example of where the checklist tool would have came in handy was we ended up moving our pad from the hospital to the airport and the temperatures were super high.

144
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:09,000
So we had to disconnect all of our equipment and a lot of that stuff was, you know, charging in the helicopter and now has to charge somewhere else.

145
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:27,000
Well, long story short, we ended up forgetting the ventilator on the charger. And we got a scene flight and had the RSI, which went perfect textbook perfect. We had a checklist for that and we read it.

146
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:48,000
It went very smooth. But when we got back to the helicopter to hook the patient up for the event, the event was still on the charger back at the base because we're brand new to this process of shuttling everything from our base to the airport to the helicopter.

147
00:30:48,000 --> 00:31:03,000
And it was a very short flight and the patient did great. So there was no harm in it. But yeah, that was a pretty big example where having a script, having a checklist definitely comes in handy.

148
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:22,000
As far as example of having this script for really, really I made it for testing purposes. So just like, okay, this is a new program with quite a few different protocols and what I'm used to.

149
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:44,000
All of the buffering came out with the practice test when I listened to the audio and I forced myself to write down the protocol. And if I didn't remember from top to bottom the protocol, then I would have to highlight those areas and do it again.

150
00:31:44,000 --> 00:32:04,000
And I really was successful in hitting pretty much all my protocols doing it that way. So yeah, all the examples of that really came through while practicing for the test before the test came.

151
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:14,000
And now I had the protocols dialed in for applying them to patients. Did that answer your question?

152
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:28,000
Yeah, for sure. Yeah, I guess I'll answer my own question with a very short example. I once I'm when I was a newer medic back in Pittsburgh, tried to pass an ET tube on a patient.

153
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:44,000
And then for whatever reason, it wasn't going through. And somebody looked over my shoulder, like, hey, man, you forgot your style. Yeah, I had a failed attempt because I didn't have a system for how I did things.

154
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:56,000
It was just kind of like a free for all. Hopefully now that wouldn't happen one because I got a lot more experience. But I also have a script to follow. And then we have a checklist that goes with that.

155
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:01,000
So that's just an example of how these things might be valuable in the field.

156
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:21,000
Yeah, for sure. And to follow up with that story, we ended up making checklists of what to grab before you take the UConn back to the pad for everybody to follow.

157
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:42,000
So we don't forget a ventilator or a battery or any number of things that like if you're if something changes up and you have to you have to do something different, it's easy to forget stuff if you don't have a checklist or if you haven't done it a dozen times before that.

158
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:53,000
Yeah, and unfortunately, I think that's how a lot of these a lot of these things come up is something bad happened, some kind of bad outcome transpired. And then we try and like fix the app effect.

159
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:58,000
Yeah, or even near misses. Hopefully more near misses.

160
00:33:58,000 --> 00:34:15,000
Yeah, but there definitely is a way to be proactive with this. And I definitely I like the idea of doing these scripts, whether for learning something new or getting familiar protocols at a new agency, or even just dialing in your technique for for something you're already good at.

161
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:18,000
So I definitely appreciate you sharing that with us.

162
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:22,000
Yeah, thanks for having me.

